@@ -0,0 +1,179 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — kiddo v6.0.0-alpha.3
+
+Property-based tests were added with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`cargo add --dev hegeltest`,
+package `hegeltest` 0.28.2, crate name `hegel`). All tests live in the existing unit-test
+module of `src/kd_tree/mod.rs`, as `kd_tree::tests::hegel_props`, and run with the default
+feature set via `cargo test --lib hegel_props`.
+
+Shared infrastructure: a `coord()` generator mixing unit-scale, mid-scale (±1e9), and
+extreme-but-finite (±f64::MAX) magnitudes plus exact special values (±0.0, ±1.0, 1e-300,
+±1e300, ±f64::MAX); a duplicate-biased point-set generator (~25% of points are exact copies
+of earlier points); query points that are 50% exact copies of tree points; radii snapped to
+(or perturbed one part in 10⁹ around) exact point distances; and a brute-force oracle built
+on the crate's own scalar `DistanceMetricScalar::dist_raw`, so distances are exactly
+comparable and the tree's traversal/pruning/collection logic is what is under test.
+Coordinates of ±inf are excluded by evidence: floats' `Axis::max_value()` (`+inf`) is the
+stem sentinel, and a `TODO` on `new_from_slice` documents that such coordinates are
+unsupported pending checked ingress APIs. NaN is outside the `Axis::cmp` domain.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+1. **`nearest_one` matches brute force — SquaredEuclidean, Manhattan, Chebyshev**
+   (`hegel_nearest_one_matches_brute_force_{squared_euclidean,manhattan,chebyshev}`).
+   Returned distance equals the true minimum; returned item is one of the items achieving
+   it. Evidence: crate docs ("Exact Nearest Neighbour"), the crate's own fuzz suite
+   (`tests/kd_tree_fuzz_v6.rs::assert_nearest_one`) asserts the same oracle. 2-D
+   `FlatVec` tree with bucket size 4, up to 200 points, so trees get several stem levels.
+
+2. **`nearest_n` matches brute force** (`hegel_nearest_n_matches_brute_force`). Length is
+   `min(n, size)`; distances equal the brute-force ranking exactly and in ascending order;
+   items are distinct; each reported distance equals the recomputed distance of the
+   reported item. Evidence: lib.rs doctest showing sorted `QueryResultItem`s; fuzz suite's
+   `compare_nearest_n_sorted`. Run on a 3-D tree (different `K` than the other tests).
+
+3. **`within` (inclusive) matches a naive filter** (`hegel_within_inclusive_matches_brute_force`).
+   Item set equals `{i : dist_i <= r}`, results ascend by distance, per-item distances are
+   true. Evidence: builder docs — default is "inclusive boundary (<= max_dist)"; results
+   "ordered by distance". Radii are snapped to exact point distances to probe the boundary.
+
+4. **`within().exclusive_boundaries()` matches a strict filter**
+   (`hegel_within_exclusive_matches_brute_force`). Item set equals `{i : dist_i < r}`.
+   Evidence: builder doc "Uses strict `< radius` semantics instead of inclusive `<= radius`".
+
+5. **`within().unsorted()` matches the inclusive filter** (`hegel_within_unsorted_matches_brute_force`).
+   Same membership through the separate unsorted traversal/collection path. Evidence:
+   builder doc "Returns results in traversal order instead of sorted-by-distance order".
+
+6. **`nearest_n(n).within(r)` matches brute force** (`hegel_nearest_n_within_matches_brute_force`).
+   Returns exactly the first `min(n, |within r|)` entries of the distance ranking, all
+   within the radius, distinct items, true distances. Evidence: crate docs "`k` items
+   within a max radius".
+
+7. **`best_n_within` keeps the smallest-item in-radius entries**
+   (`hegel_best_n_within_matches_brute_force`). `into_sorted_vec()` equals the in-radius
+   items sorted ascending by item value, truncated to `max_qty`, with true distances.
+   Evidence: builder doc "keeps the best `max_qty` entries by item ordering"; identical to
+   the fuzz suite's `expected_best_n_within` oracle.
+
+8. **Approximate nearest-one is never better than exact and reports a real entry's true
+   distance** (`hegel_approx_nearest_one_never_better_than_exact`). Evidence:
+   `approx_nearest_one` docs — "may not return the true nearest neighbour. It searches only
+   the leaf that the query point falls into" (so its result can never beat the exact one,
+   and must be an actual entry of that leaf).
+
+9. **Fixed-point tree agrees exactly with the f64 oracle on representable inputs**
+   (`hegel_fixed_point_nearest_one_agrees_with_f64_oracle`). A `FixedI32<U16>` tree over a
+   lattice of multiples of 1/256 (|coord| <= 5, so squared distances are exactly
+   representable in both types) returns the same nearest distance and a tied item.
+   Evidence: `Axis` docs list `FixedI32<U16>` as a supported coordinate type.
+
+10. **Stateful model test: mutable tree vs `Vec` model**
+    (`hegel_mutable_tree_agrees_with_vec_model`, `#[hegel::state_machine]`). Rules:
+    `add` (unique-coordinate points — see Suspected bugs #2), `remove` (biased toward
+    exact existing entries; model removes every exact `(point, item)` match, matching
+    `remove_from_leaf`), `replace_item` (asserts `Ok` iff the entry exists, else
+    `Err(EntryNotFound)`, per its docs), and a `check_nearest_one` query rule against the
+    model. Invariant after every step: `size()` equals model length and `iter()` contents
+    equal the model as a multiset. Evidence: `construction.rs` docs for
+    `add`/`remove`/`replace_item`; `VecOfArrays` is the only `MutableLeafStrategy`.
+
+Plus two deterministic known-bug pinning tests (see below), which fail on every run by
+design.
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test --lib hegel_props`: **12 passed, 2 failed** — the two failures are the
+  intentional known-bug pins (`*_known_bug` tests), each failing deterministically on
+  every generated case with an already-minimal counterexample.
+- Full default-feature suite `cargo test --tests --no-fail-fast`: **412 passed, 2 failed**
+  (the same two pins); all pre-existing tests still pass, and all other test targets
+  (`string_data_test`, `builder_ui`, `custom_*`, `donnelly_simd_regressions`) pass.
+- Liveness check: temporarily inverting the inclusive-`within` filter (`<=` → `<`)
+  produced a shrunk counterexample (single point at the origin, radius 0) — the suite can
+  fail. Reverted.
+- Exploratory 10x run: all 12 properties re-run at `test_cases = 1000` (10x the default);
+  no new failures. Committed tests use the default 100 cases.
+- During development the properties failed three times; two were real bugs (below), one
+  was an unsound assertion (below).
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+1. **`nearest_one` fabricates `T::default()` as the result item when the minimum distance
+   overflows to `+inf`.** Pinned by
+   `hegel_nearest_one_overflowed_distance_fabricates_default_item_known_bug`.
+   The leaf scan (`src/leaf_view_chunked/nearest_one.rs`) starts from
+   `best_dist = A::max_value()` (`+inf` for floats), `best_item = T::default()` and only
+   updates on strict `dist < best_dist`; `inf < inf` never holds. With finite,
+   crate-accepted coordinates such as `x = ±1e300`, the squared-euclidean distance
+   overflows to `+inf`, and the query returns distance `+inf` with an item that need not
+   exist in the tree at all (minimal repro: one entry `(item 1, [1e300, 0])`, query
+   `[-1e300, 0]` → returns item 0). `nearest_n`, `within`, and `best_n_within` handle the
+   identical inputs correctly (they return the inf-distance entries with their real
+   items), so this is specific to the nearest-one collector; the `approx()` path shares
+   it. Sibling assertions that could only fail through this root cause skip that one
+   check, with comments referencing the pin.
+
+2. **`add()` silently scrambles item/point pairings when splitting a full leaf whose
+   below-median entries all share the split-dimension coordinate.** Pinned by
+   `hegel_mutable_add_split_scrambles_items_known_bug`; found by the model test.
+   `VecOfArrays::split_leaf` (`src/leaf_strategy/vec_of_arrays.rs`) sorts the
+   split-dimension coordinates with `mirror_select_nth_unstable_by` and applies the
+   recorded permutation to the other dimensions and items. If the pivot walks down to
+   index 0 (everything below the median equals the split value), it re-sorts — but it
+   **reuses the already-populated `mirror` array**. The second call composes the second
+   permutation onto the first, and that composed permutation is then applied to arrays
+   that already had the first permutation applied: the first permutation is applied twice
+   to items/other dimensions but once to the split-dimension coordinates. Minimal repro
+   (bucket size 4): add `(0,[0,0]) (1,[0,0]) (2,[1,0]) (3,[0,0]) (4,[0,0])` — afterwards
+   the tree contains `(0,[1,0])` and `(2,[0,0])`: item 2 has lost its point. Consequences:
+   `iter()` returns entries that were never inserted, queries return wrong items, and
+   `remove`/`replace_item` can miss real entries. This is silent data corruption for any
+   mutable-tree workload with duplicate points (or merely repeated coordinate values on
+   one axis, e.g. integer-valued or grid-snapped data). The crate's own fuzz suite misses
+   it because it draws continuous random coordinates, where such collisions essentially
+   never occur. The model test's `add` rule only inserts points with per-dimension-unique
+   coordinates so it cannot re-trip this bug while it continues to test everything else
+   (comment in the rule references the pin).
+
+Not a bug (investigated and judged legitimate): a leaf split may exchange the stored
+representations of `-0.0` and `+0.0` between entries whose coordinates compare equal —
+unobservable through the tree's own `PartialOrd`/`PartialEq` semantics.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Bit-exact signed-zero preservation per entry.** The model invariant originally
+  compared tree contents to the model by raw `f64::to_bits`, and failed when a split
+  exchanged `-0.0`/`+0.0` between two equal-comparing entries. The tree never promises
+  bit-level identity of coordinates that compare equal, so the invariant now normalizes
+  `-0.0` to `+0.0` (and stays bit-exact for every other value, so a genuine pairing
+  scramble of distinct values is still caught — as bug #2 demonstrates).
+- No other property had to be weakened for soundness reasons. Exact (bitwise) distance
+  equality against the `dist_raw`-based oracle held throughout on the default scalar
+  build, including at extreme magnitudes and boundary-snapped radii, so no ULP tolerance
+  (as used by the crate's fuzz suite for SIMD paths) was needed. The two `is_finite()`
+  guards in `check_nearest_one` and the approx property are known-bug exclusions
+  referencing the pinned test, not soundness fixes.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The workflow worked well end-to-end: both real bugs were found by Tier-1 patterns from
+  the skill (the stateful model test found the data-corruption bug; extreme-magnitude
+  generator discipline found the inf-distance bug). The duplicate-point bias called out in
+  the task brief was exactly what triggered bug #2.
+- Shrinking quality was excellent: the model-test counterexample shrank to a 5-step add
+  sequence, and the inf-distance bug to a single entry; the verbose per-step `Step N:
+  <rule>` + `draw_N` output made it possible to reconstruct the exact failing sequence
+  into a deterministic unit repro without re-running.
+- The reference's gotcha about the `.hegel` failure database replaying stale
+  counterexamples was useful; `rm -rf .hegel` between generator changes avoided
+  confusion. `.hegel/` added to `.gitignore` per the reference.
+- Pain point: there is no global case-count override (reference gotcha #13), so the 10x
+  exploratory run required temporarily editing 12 attributes (scripted). An environment
+  variable like `HEGEL_TEST_CASES` would make the mandated 10x pass much cheaper.
+- Minor: `#[hegel::composite]` and plain `&TestCase` helper functions compose nicely, but
+  counterexample output for helper draws is unlabeled (`draw_N`), which cost some time
+  when reconstructing the model-test failure; per the reference this is expected, and
+  `tc.note` would have helped.
+- The kiddo repo sets `autotests = false` with explicit `[[test]]` registrations, so the
+  skill's "add to an existing test file" rule was doubly right — putting the tests in the
+  crate's existing unit-test module avoided both a new file and a `Cargo.toml` edit.
